The effective elastic moduli are found by treating the aluminum and the Kevlar‑epoxy layers separately:

*Total thickness* = \(5(0.4\;\text{mm})+4(0.2\;\text{mm})=2.8\;\text{mm}\)  

*Volume fractions*  
\[
V_{\text{Al}}=\frac{2.0}{2.8}=0.714,\qquad 
V_{\text{comp}}=\frac{0.8}{2.8}=0.286
\]

For the Kevlar‑epoxy intermediate sheet (55 % fibers, 45 % epoxy):
\[
E_{c}^{\parallel}=0.55E_{f}+0.45E_{m}=0.55(12.5\times10^{6})+0.45(0.5\times10^{6})=7.1\times10^{6}\;\text{psi}
\]
\[
\frac{1}{E_{c}^{\perp}}=\frac{0.55}{E_{f}}+\frac{0.45}{E_{m}}
\;\Rightarrow\;
E_{c}^{\perp}= \frac{E_{f}E_{m}}{0.55E_{m}+0.45E_{f}}
          \approx 1.06\times10^{6}\;\text{psi}
\]

Overall (parallel to the fibers – isostrain):
\[
E_{\parallel}=V_{\text{Al}}E_{\text{Al}}+V_{\text{comp}}E_{c}^{\parallel}
=0.714(10\times10^{6})+0.286(7.1\times10^{6})
\approx 9.2\times10^{6}\;\text{psi}
\]

Overall (perpendicular to the fibers – isostress through the thickness):
\[
\frac{1}{E_{\perp}}=\frac{V_{\text{Al}}}{E_{\text{Al}}}+\frac{V_{\text{comp}}}{E_{c}^{\perp}}
\;\Rightarrow\;
E_{\perp}\approx 2.9\times10^{6}\;\text{psi}
\]

The principal advantages of Arall over unreinforced aluminum are its higher specific strength, markedly improved fatigue and fracture‑toughness, and greater damage tolerance while retaining a modulus comparable to that of aluminum.

\[
\boxed{(9.2\times10^{6},\;2.9\times10^{6})}
\]

The transverse modulus for a continuous, aligned fiber composite is obtained from the inverse rule of mixtures:

\[
\frac{1}{E_{c}}=\frac{V_f}{E_f}+\frac{V_m}{E_m}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
E_{c}= \frac{E_f\,E_m}{V_fE_m+V_mE_f}
\]

With \(V_f=0.40\), \(E_f=69\ \text{GPa}\), \(V_m=0.60\) and \(E_m=3.4\ \text{GPa}\),

\[
E_c=\frac{69\times3.4}{0.40(3.4)+0.60(69)}
      =\frac{234.6}{1.36+41.4}
      \approx 5.5\ \text{GPa}.
\]

